Titre$ 60+ Marasmioid Mushrooms in North America
com$ adapt GF sept 2015

1,0$ source : Kuo, M. (2013, January). Marasmioid mushrooms. Retrieved from the MushroomExpert.Com Web site: http://www.mushroomexpert.com/marasmioid.html
1,100$Bibliographie
1,0$
1,2 $ Decomposing debris of plants (grasses, sedges, rushes, ferns, flowers, vines, etc.).
1,10 $ Decomposing tree litter.
2,0 $ Growing in grassy areas, in troops or fairy rings; cap 1-5 cm across and usually broadly bell-shaped, buff to tan; gills well spaced. $ Marasmius oreades
2,3 $ Cap much smaller (rarely over 15 mm across); growing in grassy areas or not.
3,0 $ On litter of salal and Oregon grape; odor of garlic; Pacific Northwest. $ Mycetinis salalis
3,4 $ Substrate different; odor not distinctive; variously distributed.
4,0 $ On litter of mountain avens or on living avens; arctic and alpine ecosystems. $ Mycetinis epidryasat CMM (photo only) @
4,5 $ Substrate different; variously distributed.
5,6 $ Fresh cap white, buff, or pale (perhaps with a slightly darker center); western species.
5,8 $ Fresh cap more highly colored; eastern and western species.
6,0 $ Mature cap to only 2 mm across, buff to pale fawn with a cinnamon center; spores 8-12 x 4-6 µ. $ Marasmius limosus
6,7 $ Not as above.(not : Mature cap to only 2 mm across, buff to pale fawn with a cinnamon center; spores 8-12 x 4-6 µ.
7,0 $ Cap pure white; spores 13-16 x 5-6.5 µ. $ Marasmius caricis
7,0 $ Cap whitish with pinkish to cinnamon tinges over the center; spores 8-9 x 4 µ. $ Marasmiellus vaillantiiat Roger's Mushrooms @
8,0 $ Base of stem without prominent basal mycelium or fuzz; spores 7-11 x 3-5 µ. $ Marasmius graminum~ M. curreyi at MykoWeb @
8,9 $ Base of stem with mycelium or fuzz; spores variously sized.
9,0 $ Pennsylvania to Louisiana and Illinois; spores 13-19 x 3.5-5 µ. $ Marasmius pseudobambusinus
9,0 $ Widely distributed; spores 8.5-10.5 x 3.5-4 µ. $ Marasmius armeniacusat MykoWeb @
10,11 $ On debris of conifers.
10,27 $ On debris of hardwoods.
11,12 $ Odor of garlic or onions, or strong and foul (crush a cap between your thumb and finger).
11,16 $ Odor not distinctive.
12,0 $ Odor strong and foul, but not of garlic or onions; on needles of spruce, fir, or pine (one or two mushrooms per needle); cap dull brown, beige, or nearly white. $ Micromphale perforans
12,13 $ Odor distinctly of garlic or onions.
13,0 $ Mature cap extremely tiny (under 3 mm across); whitish, dull pinkish, or brownish gray. I see no convincing way to separate these two species, with or without a microscope. $ Marasmiellus filopesMarasmius thujinus @
13,14 $ Mature cap larger than above (4 to 20 mm across); more highly colored.
14,0 $ Stem surface smooth. $ Mycetinis scorodonius
14,15 $ Stem surface more or less velvety.
15,0 $ Scattered or in loose troops on litter of pines (Pinus species); maximum cap size about 10 mm; stem base inserted directly into needles; spores 7.5-9 x 3-4 µ. $ Marasmius thiersii
15,0 $ In dense troops on litter of Douglas-fir, true firs (Abies species) or pines (Pinus species); maximum cap size about 20 mm; stem base not inserted directly; spores 9-10 x 5-6 µ. $ Mycetinis applanatipes
16,17 $ Fresh cap whitish to very pale grayish or faintly pinkish.
16,18 $ Fresh cap more highly colored.
17,0 $ Growing from needles; stem remaining fairly pale; spores 9-10 x 3.5-4.5 µ. $ Marasmius calhouniaeat MykoWeb @
17,0 $ Growing from twigs; stem darkening from the base up with age; spores 11-15 x 3.5-5.5 µ. $ Marasmiellus candidusat Roger's Mushrooms @
18,19 $ Fresh cap rose, pink, red, wine colored, purple-red, etc.
18,21 $ Fresh cap otherwise colored (orange, brown, orangish brown, cinnamon, rust, olive brown, yellow brown . . . ).
19,0 $ Cap conspicuously pleated; rose pink, fading to pinkish with a darker center; growing on needles east of the Rocky Mountains. $ Marasmius pulcherripes
19,20 $ Cap not conspicuously pleated; more highly colored when fresh; growing on various substrates; western.
20,0 $ Growing on well decayed wood; cap 5-20 mm across; gills often with a tiny tooth running down the stem; spores 8-13 x 3-4 µ. $ Marasmiellus papillatus
20,0 $ Growing on humus; cap 10-40 mm across; gills often nearly free; spores 11-15 x 5-6.5 µ. $ Marasmius plicatulus
21,22 $ Cap conspicuously pleated; eastern species.
21,23 $ Cap somewhat lined or not, but not conspicuously pleated; eastern and western species.
22,0 $ Fresh cap orange to brownish orange; common from about Tennessee northward; spores 16-23 x 3-4 µ; odor not distinctive; taste mild to slightly bitter; large cystidia with refractive contents present. $ Marasmius siccus
22,0 $ Fresh cap cinnamon or rusty brown; common from about Tennessee southward; spores 15-18 x 3-4.5 µ; odor and taste often mealy; cystidia absent or rare (if present, without refractive contents). $ Marasmius fulvoferrugineus
23,0 $ Cap olive brown, fading to light yellowish brown, pale olive brown, or grayish brown (the center often darker than the margin); often growing with ferns nearby; stem with a long root that extends into the substrate; spores shaped like lemons. $ Rhizomarasmius undatusat Roger's Mushrooms @= Marasmius undatus @
23,24 $ Cap without olive tones; root lacking; spores otherwise shaped.
24,0 $ Growing on debris of redwoods on the West Coast; cap brown to brownish orange or orangish, 6-12 mm wide; taste (not odor) strongly garlic-like after 1-2 minutes. $ Micromphale sequoiae
24,25 $ Not completely as above.
25,26 $ Cap more or less "brown," without orange shades.
25,0 $ Cap with orange to orange-brown shades, fading to dull apricot. $ Marasmius armeniacusat MykoWeb @
26,0 $ Cap pale brownish or yellowish brown, with a darker brown center; gills without cystidia. $ Marasmius pallidocephalus
26,0 $ Cap medium brown to dark brown; gill edges with upside-down mutant cow udders in miniature ("diverticulate" or "broom" cells) as cystidia. $ Marasmius androsaceusat Roger's Mushrooms @
27,28 $ Growing on debris of madrone, tanoak, sycamore, black locust, birch, quaking aspen, black cottonwood, or American holly. Several of the subsequent species are not repeated later in the key.
27,38 $ Growing on debris of other hardwoods.
28,0 $ Growing on petioles of sycamore leaves; cap minute, pale becoming pinkish brown. $ Marasmius felix
28,29 $ Not as above.( Not as : Growing on petioles of sycamore leaves; cap minute, pale becoming pinkish brown.
29,0 $ Growing on bark litter of madrone; cap to 1 cm across, brown; odor and taste of garlic or onions. $ Micromphale arbuticola
29,30 $ Not as above.( Not as :Growing on bark litter of madrone; cap to 1 cm across, brown; odor and taste of garlic or onions
30,0 $ Growing on litter of black locust; cap 4-13 mm across, pale orange (usually darker over the center and paler marginally), strongly or faintly pleated; recorded from Michigan but probably to be expected throughout the range of the tree. $ Marasmius robinianus
30,31 $ Not as above.( Not as :Growing on litter of black locust; cap 4-13 mm across, pale orange (usually darker over the center and paler marginally), strongly or faintly pleated
31,32 $ Growing on leaf litter of American holly; cap minute, pale with a darker brownish center.
31,33 $ Not as above.( Not as : Growing on leaf litter of American holly; cap minute, pale with a darker brownish center.
32,0 $ Gills attached by means of a collar that encircles the stem. $ Marasmius ilicicola
32,0 $ Gills attached directly to the stem. $ Marasmius ilicis
33,34 $ Growing on debris of tanoak.
33,36 $ Growing on debris of birch, quaking aspen, or black cottonwood.
34,0 $ Odor of garlic or onions; cap 5-10 mm across, dark reddish brown. $ Marasmius thiersii
34,35 $ Odor not distinctive; other features variable.
35,0 $ Cap to 6 mm across, dark brown fading to light brown. $ Marasmius quercophilusat MykoWeb @
35,38 $ Not as above.( Not as : Cap to 6 mm across, dark brown fading to light brown.
36,37 $ Cap tiny (5 mm across maximum) and whitish.
36,38 $ Not as above.( Not as : Cap tiny (5 mm across maximum) and whitish.
37,0 $ Growing on leaves of birch or quaking aspen; spores 8-11 µ long; basidia 4-spored. $ Marasmius epiphyllusat Roger's Mushrooms @
37,0 $ Growing on leaves of black cottonwood (Populus trichocarpa); spores 11-14 µ long; basidia 2-spored. $ Marasmius tremulae
38,39 $ Odor of garlic or onions (crush a cap between your thumb and finger).
38,41 $ Odor not distinctive, somewhat foul, spermatic, bleachlike, mealy, or radishlike--but not of garlic.
39,0 $ Stem bald; most spores under 10 µ long; cheilocystidia digitate with many fingerlike projections. $ Mycetinis scorodonius
39,40 $ Stem hairy to finely velvety; most spores longer than 10 µ; cheilocystidia lumpy and lobed, with only a few, vaguely fingerlike projections.
40,0 $ Found in western North America. $ Mycetinis copelandiiat MykoWeb @=Marasmius copelandii @
40,0 $ Found east of the Rocky Mountains. $ Mycetinis olidusat Roger's Mushrooms @= Marasmius olidus @
41,42 $ Fresh cap rose pink, red, purplish red, or wine colored.
41,46 $ Fresh cap otherwise colored.
42,43 $ Found in western North America.
42,44 $ Found east of the Rocky Mountains.
43,0 $ Growing on well decayed wood; cap .5-2 cm across; gills often with a tiny tooth running down the stem; spores 8-13 x 3-4 µ. $ Marasmiellus papillatus
43,0 $ Growing on humus; cap 1-4 cm across; gills often nearly free; spores 11-15 x 5-6.5 µ. $ Marasmius plicatulus
44,0 $ Cap rose pink, fading to pinkish with a darker center. $ Marasmius pulcherripes
44,45 $ Cap purple red to red, perhaps fading to reddish brown.
45,0 $ Gills pinkish, often with red edges; spores 16-22 x 4-5.5 µ. $ Marasmius haematocephalusat Badidiomycetes of Greater Antilles @
45,0 $ Gills whitish to yellowish, the edges colored like the faces; spores 8.5-12 x 3-4 µ. $ Marasmius bellipes
46,47 $ Mature cap regularly at least 2-3 cm across (often even larger); stem not wiry. (If cap is 2-3 cm across and the stem is not wiry, try this choice first.)
46,53 $ Mature cap rarely as wide as 2-3 cm; stem wiry or not.
47,0 $ Cap whitish with a dark brown or reddish brown center; found in eastern North America. $ Marasmius nigrodiscus
47,48 $ Not as above.( Not as : Cap whitish with a dark brown or reddish brown center; found in eastern North America.
48,49 $ Entire stem remaining pale (whitish to yellow or very pale brownish) throughout development.
48,50 $ At least the lower portion of stem dark (brown, reddish brown, orange-brown, etc.) by maturity.
49,0 $ Growing from leaf litter; cystidia present only on gill edges. $ Marasmius strictipes
49,0 $ Growing from leaf litter, twigs, or logs; cystidia present on gill faces and edges. $ Marasmius cystidiosusat Roger's Mushrooms @
50,51 $ Fresh cap pale (whitish, yellowish, pinkish cinnamon).
50,52 $ Fresh cap some shade of brown.
51,0 $ Mature cap whitish with a very slightly darker center, becoming somewhat wrinkled; gills with dextrinoid cystidia (setae). $ Marasmius delectans
51,0 $ Mature cap pinkish cinnamon with a paler margin, not usually wrinkled; gills with colorless cystidia and broom cells. $ Marasmius spissus
52,0 $ Often growing in clusters of 2-3 mushrooms; gills with dextrinoid cystidia (setae). $ Marasmius cohaerens
52,0 $ Usually growing scattered to gregariously; gills with colorless to yellowish cystidia and broom cells. $ Marasmius floridanus
53,54 $ Fresh cap white or nearly so (pale yellowish, buff, cream).
53,63 $ Fresh cap more highly colored.
54,55 $ Cap conspicuously pleated.
54,56 $ Cap smooth to wrinkled or faintly lined, but not conspicuously pleated.
55,0 $ Usually growing on sticks, woody debris, and logs; cap at maturity with a flat top in side view. $ Marasmius rotula
55,0 $ Usually growing on leaves; cap at maturity evenly rounded-convex in side view. $ Marasmius capillaris
56,0 $ Cap minute (2 mm maximum width); growing on leaves of American beech. $ Marasmius epifagus
56,57 $ Not as above.(not :  Cap minute (2 mm maximum width); growing on leaves of American beech.)
57,58 $ Stem surface smooth or minutely pubescent.
57,59 $ Stem surface finely to prominently hairy (use a hand lens on fresh specimens).
58,0 $ Cap 2-13 mm across; usually growing on sedges, rushes, ferns (etc.) but also recorded on twigs of alder, willow, beech, tulip poplar, sycamore, pine, and hemlock; cystidia not dextrinoid. $ Marasmiellus vaillantiiat Roger's Mushrooms @
58,0 $ Cap 7-40 mm across; usually growing on twigs or leaves of oaks, but recorded on debris of other hardwoods; cystidia dextrinoid. $ Marasmius delectans
59,60 $ Entire stem remaining whitish or pale brownish over the course of development.
59,61 $ At least the bottom portion of the stem darkening to brown, dark brown, or black with maturity.
60,0 $ Growing from hardwood leaves or leaf stems, or from grasses or vines; pileipellis a hymeniform layer of broom cells. $ Marasmius falcatipes
60,0 $ Growing from hardwood sticks; pileipellis a cutis with lobed or slightly diverticulate terminal elements. $ Mycetinis opacus
61,0 $ Stem soon black except at the apex; spores triangular to jack-shaped. $ Tetrapyrgos nigripes
61,62 $ Stem becoming brown to dark brown (if blackish, only at the base); spores ellipsoid to pip-shaped.
62,0 $ Threadlike black rhizomorphs often found in the substrate (sticks); gills sometimes discoloring orangish with age; pileipellis with setae; spores 13-20 µ long. $ Marasmius tenerrius var. setulosis
62,0 $ Rhizomorphs absent; gills not discoloring orangish; pileipellis without setae; spores 8-13 µ long. $ Marasmius epiphyllusat Roger's Mushrooms @
63,64 $ Color of fresh cap ranging from orange to orange-brown to rusty or reddish brown.
63,72 $ Color of fresh cap ranging from brown to light brown to yellow-brown to tan or olive brown.
64,65 $ Mature cap conspicuously pleated or prominently lined.
64,69 $ Mature cap smooth or faintly lined, but not conspicuously lined or pleated.
65,0 $ Spores under 12 µ in length; cap initially brown to yellow-brown but sometimes fading to orangish brown; growing on leaves. $ Marasmius glabellus
65,66 $ Spores longer than 12 µ; cap with orange or rusty shades from the beginning; growing on leaves or woody debris.
66,0 $ Cap 3-8 mm across; rare; documented from Tennessee. $ Marasmius haematocephalus var. anomaloides
66,67 $ Cap larger than above; common throughout eastern North America.
67,0 $ Found in northeastern North America from roughly the 43rd parallel northwards; many spores under 15 µ in length; odor reminiscent of Pleurotus ostreatus. $ Marasmius borealis
67,68 $ Found south of the 43rd parallel or perhaps above it; nearly all spores longer than 15 µ; odor not distinctive or mealy.
68,0 $ Fresh cap orange to brownish orange; common from about Tennessee northward; spores 16-23 x 3-4 µ; odor not distinctive; taste mild to slightly bitter; large cystidia with refractive contents present. $ Marasmius siccus
68,0 $ Fresh cap cinnamon or rusty brown; common from about Tennessee southward; spores 15-18 x 3-4.5 µ; odor and taste often mealy; cystidia absent or rare (if present, without refractive contents). $ Marasmius fulvoferrugineus
69,0 $ Stem densely hairy, long and usually rooting somewhat; cap cuticle without broom cells. $ Rhizomarasmius pyrrhocephalus
69,70 $ Stem smooth or very finely hairy, usually not rooting; cap cuticle with broom cells.
70,0 $ Found in northeastern North America from roughly the 43rd parallel northwards; spores over 12 µ in length; odor reminiscent of Pleurotus ostreatus. $ Marasmius borealis
70,71 $ Widely distributed; spores shorter than 12 µ; odor not distinctive.
71,0 $ Apparently limited to eastern North America; cap 6-25 mm across, bright orange or dark orange-brown when fresh; broom cells and cystidia present on gills. $ Marasmius sullivantii
71,0 $ Apparently widely distributed in North America; cap 4-12 mm across, orange to orangish but rarely bright orange; broom cells present on gills but not cystidia. $ Marasmius armeniacusat MykoWeb @
72,73 $ Basal half of stem remaining fairly pale through maturity.
72,74 $ At least the basal half of the stem dark brown to black by maturity.
73,0 $ Stem usually eccentric (sometimes central); stature minute (cap 1-7 mm across, stem under 3 mm long). $ Marasmius falcatipes
73,0 $ Stem usually central (sometimes slightly erratic); small but not minute (cap 3-24 mm across, stem 26-40 mm long). $ Marasmius insipidus
74,0 $ Usually growing on curled, blackened ash leaves (but recorded on elm, poplar, and other hardwood leaves); cap minute (under 4 mm across), grayish brown to light brown. $ Marasmius minutus
74,75 $ Not completely as above. (not : Usually growing on curled, blackened ash leaves (but recorded on elm, poplar, and other hardwood leaves); cap minute (under 4 mm across), grayish brown to light brown
75,0 $ Growing on leaves of oaks or tanoak in western North America; cap minute (2-5 mm across), light brown over the center and paler marginally. $ Marasmius quercophilusat MykoWeb @
75,76 $ Not completely as above. (not : Growing on leaves of oaks or tanoak in western North America; cap minute (2-5 mm across), light brown over the center and paler marginally.
76,0 $ Stem quite long (60-100 mm long), with a scurfy surface. $ Marasmius chordalis
76,77 $ Stem shorter than above (between 5 and 65 mm long), with a smooth to faintly hairy or pubescent surface.
77,0 $ Spores under 12 µ long. $ Marasmius glabellus
77,78 $ Spores over 13 µ long.
78,0 $ Cap conspicuously pleated at maturity; stem smooth; odor spermatic; spores 16-24 µ long. $ Marasmius paludigenus
78,0 $ Cap wrinkled or somewhat lined along the margin at maturity, but not conspicuously pleated; stem faintly hairy or pubescent; odor not distinctive; spores 13.5-18 µ long. $ Marasmius ciliatomarginatus


